{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Typical anemia symptoms of vitamin B12 deficiency include fatigue, paleness, shortness of breath, headaches, and dizziness.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Neurologic signs may appear later and can involve tingling sensations or pain, trouble walking, and uncontrollable muscle movements.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Cognitive changes such as confusion, slower thinking, forgetfulness, memory loss, depression, or irritability are also reported.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Additional possible symptoms are problems with smell or taste, vision disturbances, diarrhea and weight loss, and a painful smooth red tongue (glossitis).",
      "sources": [1]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Vitamin B12 is naturally present in animal‑derived foods such as meat, fish, dairy, and eggs, and strict vegans avoid these sources.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Because the body cannot make vitamin B12, people who do not consume animal products must obtain it from fortified foods or supplements to meet their needs.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "If adequate B12 is not obtained, a deficiency can develop, leading to anemia and other health problems.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": false,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "The provided sources note that the recommended daily amount of vitamin B12 varies based on age, sex, and pregnancy status, but they do not specify the exact amount needed for pregnant women.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "They advise that a healthcare provider can determine the appropriate vitamin B12 intake for an individual based on medical history.",
      "sources": [1]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Good sources of iron include beans, dried fruits, eggs, lean red meat, salmon, iron‑fortified breads and cereals, peas, tofu, and dark green leafy vegetables.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Iron is also found naturally in lean meat, seafood, poultry, white beans, lentils, spinach, kidney beans, peas, nuts, and some dried fruits such as raisins.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "Iron‑fortified breakfast cereals and breads are effective ways to increase dietary iron intake.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "Including vitamin C‑rich foods like oranges, strawberries, or tomatoes with iron‑containing meals can improve iron absorption.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Avoiding foods that inhibit iron absorption, such as black tea, can help maintain adequate iron levels.",
      "sources": [5]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "The most common treatment for iron‑deficiency anemia is oral iron supplements, which usually require three to six months to restore iron levels.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "If oral therapy is not tolerated or anemia is severe, intravenous iron can be given in one or a few sessions to quickly raise iron stores.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Erythropoiesis‑stimulating agents may be used together with iron therapy in people who have chronic conditions such as kidney disease.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Blood transfusions are reserved for serious cases to rapidly increase red blood cells and iron.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Surgery may be required to stop internal bleeding that is causing the iron loss.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Doctors also recommend eating iron‑rich foods like beans, lean red meat, salmon, fortified cereals, tofu, and dark leafy greens, and using vitamin C‑rich foods to enhance absorption.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Prescription iron supplements are used only when iron deficiency is confirmed, because excess iron can be harmful.",
      "sources": [4]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Heavy menstrual bleeding is listed as a condition that can lead to anemia.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Blood loss, including loss from heavy periods, reduces the amount of iron in the body, which can cause iron‑deficiency anemia.",
      "sources": [1, 4]
    },
    {
      "text": "The risk of anemia is higher for women during their menstrual periods because they may lose more blood than they replace.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "If menstrual bleeding is unusually heavy—such as needing to change pads or tampons more often than every two hours or passing large clots—it may increase the chance of developing anemia.",
      "sources": [1]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "During pregnancy a woman's blood volume expands to support the growing baby, which increases the need for iron and other nutrients.",
      "sources": [1, 4]
    },
    {
      "text": "If the increased iron requirement is not met, iron‑deficiency anemia can develop, making anemia common in pregnancy.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Because all pregnant women are at risk for anemia, doctors routinely check blood counts at multiple stages of pregnancy.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "If iron‑deficiency anemia is not diagnosed or treated, it can lead to serious complications such as persistent fatigue, headaches, and restless‑legs syndrome.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Untreated anemia may also cause heart problems, including an increased workload on the heart and possible heart failure.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "During pregnancy, lack of treatment can result in complications for both mother and baby, including higher risk of preterm birth and low birth weight.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Children who do not receive treatment can experience developmental delays and impaired growth.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "In addition, iron‑deficiency anemia can worsen other chronic conditions and make their treatments less effective.",
      "sources": [1]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Hemochromatosis is a condition in which excess iron builds up in the body’s tissues and organs.",
      "sources": [5]
    },
    {
      "text": "It can be inherited (primary or hereditary) due to gene mutations that increase iron absorption, or it can be secondary to factors such as excessive dietary iron or repeated blood transfusions.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "A rare form, neonatal hemochromatosis, occurs when a fetus’s liver is injured in utero, leading to iron overload in the newborn.",
      "sources": [2, 4]
    },
    {
      "text": "Doctors usually suspect the disorder based on a medical and family history, a physical exam, and blood tests that measure iron levels.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "If confirmed, treatment often involves regular phlebotomy (removing about a pint of blood) to lower iron stores and prevent complications.",
      "sources": [3]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Blood tests used to detect iron overload include measuring the amount of iron in the blood.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "They also assess transferrin, the protein that carries iron, and calculate the iron‑to‑transferrin saturation ratio.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "Ferritin, the protein that stores iron in the liver, is measured because high ferritin levels are typical in people with hemochromatosis.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "A high iron‑to‑transferrin ratio or elevated ferritin can suggest iron overload and prompt further evaluation.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "Genetic testing for HFE gene mutations (such as C282Y) is also ordered to confirm primary hemochromatosis, though this is a DNA test rather than a routine blood chemistry test.",
      "sources": [3]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Vitamin D deficiency means the body does not have enough vitamin D to stay healthy.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "It can result from not getting enough vitamin D in the diet, poor absorption from food, insufficient sunlight exposure, or the liver or kidneys not converting it to its active form.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "People at higher risk include breastfed infants, older adults, and individuals with dark skin because they receive less vitamin D from sunlight.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Additional risk groups are those with conditions that impair nutrient absorption such as Crohn's disease, ulcerative colitis, celiac disease, and those who have had gastric bypass surgery.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Obesity, chronic kidney or liver disease, and use of certain medications (cholesterol‑lowering, anti‑seizure, steroid, and weight‑loss drugs) also increase the chance of deficiency.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "People who are pregnant or breastfeeding may need extra vitamin D and are considered at higher risk if they have any of the above factors.",
      "sources": [3]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Severe vitamin D deficiency in children can cause rickets, a condition where the bones become soft and bend.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Vitamin D deficiency reduces calcium absorption, leading to loss of bone density that can contribute to weak bones and fractures.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Children with rickets may experience bone pain and deformities such as bowed legs or a curved spine.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Adequate vitamin D is needed for bone mineralization, so insufficient levels impair the hardening of the growing skeleton.",
      "sources": [2]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Vitamin K helps the body make proteins that are needed for healthy bones and tissues.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "It also produces proteins that are essential for blood clotting, preventing excessive bleeding.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "The body can obtain vitamin K from green vegetables, dark berries, and also from bacteria in the intestines.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Vitamin K is one of the 13 essential vitamins that the body needs for normal growth and development.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "While most vitamin K comes from food, the body can also make small amounts on its own.",
      "sources": [2]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Folic acid is a B vitamin that helps the body make healthy new cells and is especially important for women who may become pregnant.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Getting enough folic acid before and during pregnancy can prevent major birth defects of the baby’s brain or spine, known as neural tube defects.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Women are advised to consume 400 µg of folic acid each day before pregnancy and 600 µg per day during pregnancy and while breastfeeding.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Because it is difficult to obtain the recommended amount from food alone, most women will need a supplement that contains folic acid.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Good food sources of folic acid include leafy green vegetables, fruits, dried beans, peas, nuts, and fortified breads, cereals, and other grain products.",
      "sources": [2]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Vitamin C is found in many fruits and vegetables.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Good sources include citrus fruits, red and green peppers, tomatoes, broccoli, and leafy greens.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Some juices and fortified cereals also contain added vitamin C.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Vitamin C‑rich foods such as oranges, strawberries, and tomatoes can also help the body absorb iron.",
      "sources": [3]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Vitamin A is essential for vision, helping the eyes adapt to low light and preventing night blindness.",
      "sources": [1, 4]
    },
    {
      "text": "It supports bone growth and development, contributing to a healthy skeletal system.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Vitamin A plays a role in reproduction and normal fetal development.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "It is important for cell differentiation and the maintenance of healthy skin and mucous membranes.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "The vitamin also enhances immune function, helping the body fight infections.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "As an antioxidant, vitamin A helps protect cells from damage caused by free radicals.",
      "sources": [1]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Most adults need about 1,000 mg of calcium per day, but women aged 51‑70 and all adults 71 years and older should aim for 1,200 mg.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Dairy products such as milk, cheese, and yogurt are the primary sources of calcium for many people in the United States.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Calcium is also found in leafy green vegetables like kale and broccoli, in fish with soft bones such as canned sardines and salmon, and in calcium‑enriched foods such as fortified cereals, fruit juices, soy or rice drinks, and tofu.",
      "sources": [5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Supplements, including calcium carbonate and calcium citrate, provide additional calcium when dietary intake is insufficient.",
      "sources": [5]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Osteoporosis is a disease in which bones become weak and are more likely to fracture because bone mineral density and bone mass decrease, often without symptoms until a break occurs.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "The condition develops when the body breaks down more bone than it replaces, leading to loss of bone mass and changes in bone structure, especially with aging.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "Risk factors include older age, being female (especially post‑menopausal), slim body size, certain races, family history, low hormone levels, and lifestyle factors such as smoking, heavy alcohol use, and physical inactivity.",
      "sources": [1, 3]
    },
    {
      "text": "A diet low in calcium and vitamin D or lacking adequate protein can raise the risk of osteoporosis.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Conversely, a healthy, balanced diet that provides enough calcium, vitamin D, and protein is recommended to help keep bones strong and may help prevent osteoporosis.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Regular weight‑bearing and strength‑training exercise, along with limiting alcohol and not smoking, further supports bone health and reduces fracture risk.",
      "sources": [2]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Sodium helps nerves, muscles, and fluid balance, but excess sodium can build up in the blood and raise blood pressure.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "High blood pressure is a risk factor for heart disease, stroke, and other health problems.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Most U.S. adults consume more sodium than needed, so the Dietary Guidelines advise limiting intake to less than 2,300 mg per day (about one teaspoon of salt).",
      "sources": [1, 4]
    },
    {
      "text": "People who are older, have high blood pressure, diabetes, kidney disease, or are African American may be especially sensitive to sodium and should aim for lower amounts.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Reducing sodium can help keep blood pressure in a healthier range and lower the risk of related complications.",
      "sources": [4]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Potassium is a mineral and electrolyte that helps nerves function and muscles contract, including the heart muscle.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "It assists in keeping the heartbeat regular and supports overall heart rhythm.",
      "sources": [1, 5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Potassium moves nutrients into cells and waste products out of cells, aiding cellular function.",
      "sources": [1, 5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Adequate potassium intake can offset some of sodium’s harmful effects on blood pressure.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Together with other electrolytes, potassium helps balance water in the body and maintain stable blood pressure.",
      "sources": [2, 5]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Malnutrition means the body is not getting enough calories or the right balance of nutrients to stay healthy.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "It can involve undernutrition, overnutrition, or an imbalance of nutrients such as insufficient protein, vitamins, or minerals.",
      "sources": [1, 3]
    },
    {
      "text": "Common signs include low body weight, slow growth in children, frequent infections, fatigue, weakness, and skin or hair changes.",
      "sources": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "text": "People at higher risk are children, older adults, and those with chronic illnesses, low income, limited food access, or conditions that affect absorption or appetite.",
      "sources": [1, 3]
    },
    {
      "text": "Diagnosis involves nutritional screening, physical exams, and blood tests, and treatment focuses on improving diet, replacing missing nutrients, and addressing underlying causes.",
      "sources": [2]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "As people age, they often need fewer calories but must still obtain enough nutrients, and some older adults require more protein.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Changes such as living alone, limited mobility, or health problems can make it harder for seniors to shop, cook, or feed themselves.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Medications commonly used by older adults can alter taste, cause dry mouth, or suppress appetite, further reducing food intake.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Older adults are at higher risk for vitamin D deficiency because their skin produces less vitamin D from sunlight and their kidneys are less able to convert it to the active form.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "These factors increase the likelihood of malnutrition, which the sources identify older adults as a high‑risk group.",
      "sources": [4]
    },
    {
      "text": "Malnutrition in seniors can lead to fatigue, weakness, dizziness, and unintended weight loss.",
      "sources": [4]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Nutritional support is therapy for people who cannot get enough nourishment by eating or drinking.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "It may be needed if a person cannot swallow, has problems with appetite, is severely malnourished, or cannot absorb nutrients through the digestive system.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Nutritional support is provided through a feeding tube that goes into the stomach or via a needle or catheter placed in a vein.",
      "sources": [1]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Short bowel syndrome occurs when a significant portion of the small intestine is missing or non‑functional, so the remaining intestine cannot absorb enough nutrients, water, and electrolytes.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "This malabsorption can lead to dehydration, low vitamin and mineral levels, malnutrition, and growth problems in children.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treatment focuses on nutrition support, which may include enteral feeding (tube‑delivered liquid nutrition) or parenteral nutrition (IV nutrients) to provide the needed calories, protein, fats, vitamins, and minerals.",
      "sources": [1, 5]
    },
    {
      "text": "The goal of nutrition therapy is to ensure adequate nutrient intake, prevent complications, and eventually reduce dependence on parenteral nutrition.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "When the intestines stop adapting, patients may enter a maintenance stage; if absorption remains insufficient, this is called chronic intestinal failure and may require long‑term parenteral nutrition or surgery.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Long‑term parenteral nutrition carries risks such as bone loss, gallstones, liver disease, kidney stones, and catheter‑related problems.",
      "sources": [4]
    },
    {
      "text": "A registered dietitian can help tailor nutrition plans and monitor nutrient status throughout the different stages of the condition.",
      "sources": [5]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Body mass index (BMI) is calculated from a person’s weight and height to estimate whether they are underweight, normal weight, overweight, or have obesity.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "For adults ages 20 and older, a BMI of 25.0 to 29.9 is classified as overweight.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "A BMI of 30.0 or higher is classified as obesity, with a BMI of 40.0 or higher indicating severe obesity.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "BMI does not directly measure body fat, so it may misclassify very muscular people, older adults, or some ethnic groups.",
      "sources": [2, 4]
    },
    {
      "text": "Higher BMI values are associated with an increased risk for health problems such as heart disease, diabetes, and high blood pressure.",
      "sources": [3, 5]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Obesity increases the risk of type 2 diabetes, heart disease, high blood pressure, stroke, metabolic syndrome, osteoarthritis, and sleep apnea.",
      "sources": [5]
    },
    {
      "text": "It also raises the chance of developing certain cancers.",
      "sources": [5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Obesity is linked to fertility problems in both men and women, including lower sperm count and menstrual cycle disturbances.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "During pregnancy, obesity raises the risk of gestational diabetes, preeclampsia, need for cesarean delivery, and complications for both mother and baby.",
      "sources": [4]
    },
    {
      "text": "Obesity can affect mental health, increasing long‑term stress, body‑image issues, low self‑esteem, depression, and eating disorders.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Certain endocrine disorders such as polycystic ovary syndrome, Cushing’s syndrome, and hypothyroidism can be associated with obesity.",
      "sources": [3]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Treatment plans for adult obesity can include dietary changes and exercise goals to help adopt healthy nutrition, activity, and lifestyle habits.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Counseling or support groups are also recommended to provide encouragement and address underlying reasons for weight changes.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Weight‑loss medicines may be prescribed as part of an obesity treatment plan.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Weight‑loss surgery (metabolic and bariatric surgery) is an option for adults with a BMI of 40 or more, or a BMI of 35 or more with serious obesity‑related health problems, or a BMI of 30 or more with difficult‑to‑control type 2 diabetes.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Surgery is considered when nonsurgical methods such as lifestyle changes or medication have not achieved sufficient weight loss and the individual is willing to commit to lifelong dietary, activity, and medical follow‑up requirements.",
      "sources": [2]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Adults with a body‑mass index (BMI) of 40 or higher are considered good candidates for weight‑loss (metabolic and bariatric) surgery.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Adults with a BMI of 35 or higher who also have serious obesity‑related health problems such as type 2 diabetes, heart disease, or sleep apnea may qualify for surgery.",
      "sources": [2, 1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Adults with a BMI of 30‑35 who have type 2 diabetes that is difficult to control with medication and lifestyle changes can also be considered for surgery.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Candidates must have been unable to lose weight or keep it off using nonsurgical methods such as diet, exercise, or medication.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "They should understand the operation, its risks and benefits, and be prepared to adopt lifelong healthy eating, physical activity, medical follow‑up, and vitamin/mineral supplementation.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Teens may be candidates if they have a BMI of 40, or a BMI of 35‑39.9 with serious obesity‑related health problems, and are evaluated by a multidisciplinary pediatric team.",
      "sources": [3]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "People who have gastric bypass surgery often do not absorb enough nutrients, especially if they do not take prescribed vitamins and minerals.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Insufficient nutrient absorption can lead to anemia.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Lack of adequate calcium and vitamin D absorption can increase the risk of osteoporosis.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Gastric bypass patients have a higher chance of vitamin and iron shortages compared with gastric band or sleeve procedures.",
      "sources": [4]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Metabolic syndrome is a collection of risk factors that increase the chance of heart disease, diabetes, and stroke.",
      "sources": [1, 4]
    },
    {
      "text": "A person is considered to have metabolic syndrome when they have at least three of the following: a large waistline (abdominal obesity), high triglyceride levels, low HDL cholesterol, high blood pressure, and high fasting blood glucose.",
      "sources": [2, 4]
    },
    {
      "text": "The condition is closely linked to insulin resistance, where the body cannot use insulin properly, leading to higher blood sugar levels.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Major contributors include overweight or obesity and an inactive lifestyle, which together promote the development of the syndrome.",
      "sources": [4, 5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Risk increases with age, certain ethnic backgrounds (e.g., Mexican Americans), a family history of diabetes, having diabetes themselves, and women with polycystic ovary syndrome.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Most of the individual risk factors have no obvious symptoms, although a large waistline can be noticeable.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Prevention and management focus on heart‑healthy lifestyle changes such as weight control, regular physical activity, and a balanced diet, and may also involve medication to control cholesterol or blood pressure if needed.",
      "sources": [2, 3]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "The first‑line treatment for metabolic syndrome is a heart‑healthy lifestyle that includes a balanced eating plan low in saturated and trans fats, plenty of fruits, vegetables, whole grains, and lean proteins.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "Achieving and maintaining a healthy weight through calorie control and regular physical activity is emphasized as a core strategy.",
      "sources": [3, 5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Managing stress, quitting smoking, and increasing daily activity are also recommended components of the lifestyle approach.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "If lifestyle changes are insufficient, medications may be prescribed to lower blood pressure, improve cholesterol levels, or control blood sugar.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "In some cases, weight‑loss surgery can be considered when other interventions have not achieved adequate weight reduction.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Treatment may also involve addressing other health conditions that contribute to or worsen metabolic syndrome, such as diabetes or hormonal disorders.",
      "sources": [2]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Metabolic syndrome is caused by a combination of factors that include both controllable and non‑controllable elements such as diet, physical activity, age, and genetics.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Excess weight, especially abdominal fat, is a major cause because fat cells release free fatty acids that promote insulin resistance and raise “bad” LDL cholesterol while lowering “good” HDL cholesterol.",
      "sources": [3, 4]
    },
    {
      "text": "Insulin resistance, driven by free fatty acids and inflammation from immune cells, contributes to higher blood pressure, triglycerides, and abnormal cholesterol levels.",
      "sources": [3]
    },
    {
      "text": "Certain medical conditions that cannot be controlled—such as polycystic ovary syndrome, low birth weight with rapid infant weight gain, psoriasis, and some cancer treatments—also increase risk.",
      "sources": [4]
    },
    {
      "text": "Lifestyle factors you can control, like an inactive lifestyle and poor diet, further raise the risk of developing metabolic syndrome.",
      "sources": [5, 1]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Celiac disease is a chronic digestive and immune disorder that damages the small intestine.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "It is triggered by eating foods that contain gluten, a protein found naturally in wheat, barley, and rye.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "The condition can cause long‑lasting digestive problems and prevent the body from absorbing essential nutrients.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Celiac disease can also affect other parts of the body beyond the small intestine.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "It is different from gluten sensitivity, which does not damage the small intestine, and from wheat allergy, which involves a different immune response and does not cause long‑term intestinal damage.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Research indicates that celiac disease occurs only in people who have certain genetic markers and who consume gluten.",
      "sources": [4]
    },
    {
      "text": "Approximately 1 % of people worldwide, or about 2 million in the United States, are estimated to have celiac disease, though many remain undiagnosed.",
      "sources": [5]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Hidden gluten can be present in non‑food items such as children’s modeling dough, cosmetics, lipstick, lip gloss, lip balm, skin and hair products, toothpaste, mouthwash, and communion wafers.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "In foods, gluten naturally occurs in wheat (including durum, emmer, semolina, spelt), barley (found in malt, malt extract, malt vinegar, brewer’s yeast), rye, and triticale.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Processed foods may contain gluten‑derived ingredients like modified food starch, malt flavoring, preservatives, and stabilizers, which can appear in candy, condiments, hot dogs, sausages, ice cream, salad dressing, and soups.",
      "sources": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "text": "Gluten can also be hidden in many packaged foods that use grain‑based colorings, flavorings, starches, or thickeners, so boxed, canned, frozen, or prepared items may contain gluten even if they do not look like typical grain products.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Reading ingredient labels for wheat, barley, rye, or related additives and checking for gluten‑free labeling helps identify hidden sources.",
      "sources": [4]
    }
  ]
}

{
