}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "People with lactose intolerance can still meet calcium needs by eating calcium‑rich foods that do not contain lactose, such as canned salmon or sardines with soft bones, broccoli, leafy greens, oranges, almonds, Brazil nuts, dried beans, tofu, and fortified products like certain cereals, fruit juices, and soy milk.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Vitamin D, which helps the body absorb calcium, should also be included through foods like eggs, fatty fish, fortified cereals, fortified orange juice, and safe sun exposure.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "If small amounts of milk are tolerated, choosing low‑lactose options such as yogurt, hard cheeses, or milk with added vitamin D can contribute to calcium intake.",
      "sources": [4]
    },
    {
      "text": "Lactase enzyme products can be used to help digest lactose when consuming dairy, allowing occasional milk or dairy intake for calcium.",
      "sources": [4]
    },
    {
      "text": "When dietary sources are insufficient, a calcium supplement may be needed, but it should be discussed with a doctor or dietitian.",
      "sources": [3, 2]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
