{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Iron‑deficiency anemia is a type of anemia that occurs when the body does not have enough iron.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "It is the most common form of anemia.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Without sufficient iron, the body cannot produce enough healthy red blood cells to carry oxygen to tissues.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Mild or moderate cases may have no noticeable symptoms, while more serious cases can cause fatigue, shortness of breath, chest pain, dizziness, cold extremities, and pale skin.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "If left untreated, iron‑deficiency anemia can lead to complications such as headaches, restless‑leg syndrome, heart problems, pregnancy complications, and developmental delays in children.",
      "sources": [4]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "The most common treatment for iron‑deficiency anemia is oral iron supplements, which usually require three to six months to restore iron levels.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "If oral supplements are not tolerated or rapid repletion is needed, intravenous iron can be given in one or a few sessions, especially for serious cases or those with chronic conditions.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Erythropoiesis‑stimulating agents may be used together with iron therapy for patients who have both iron‑deficiency anemia and another chronic disease such as kidney disease.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Blood transfusions are reserved for severe iron‑deficiency anemia to quickly increase red blood cells and iron levels.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Surgery may be required to stop internal bleeding that is causing the iron loss.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Doctors also recommend dietary changes, encouraging iron‑rich foods like beans, lean red meat, leafy greens, and vitamin‑C‑rich foods to improve iron absorption.",
      "sources": [2]
    }
  ]
}

{
