{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Lactose intolerance is a condition in which digestive symptoms such as bloating, diarrhea, and gas occur after consuming foods or drinks that contain lactose.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "The symptoms result from lactose malabsorption, where the small intestine does not produce enough lactase to break down lactose.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Undigested lactose passes into the colon, where bacteria ferment it, creating excess fluid and gas that cause the uncomfortable symptoms.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Lactose intolerance is different from a milk allergy, which is an immune‑system reaction to milk proteins and can be life‑threatening.",
      "sources": [1, 3, 4]
    },
    {
      "text": "Most people with lactose intolerance can tolerate some amount of lactose, but the amount that can be consumed without symptoms varies among individuals.",
      "sources": [1]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "To diagnose lactose intolerance, a doctor first asks about the person's digestive symptoms, family and medical history, and eating habits.",
      "sources": [4]
    },
    {
      "text": "The doctor may also perform a physical exam and order tests to rule out other conditions that can cause similar symptoms, such as irritable bowel syndrome, celiac disease, inflammatory bowel disease, or small‑bowel bacterial overgrowth.",
      "sources": [4]
    },
    {
      "text": "One common approach is to have the patient stop consuming milk and milk products for a period to see if symptoms improve, and if they do not, further testing may be pursued.",
      "sources": [4]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Weight‑loss surgery, also called metabolic and bariatric surgery, is a group of operations that help you lose weight by changing your digestive system.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Some procedures make the stomach smaller so you feel full sooner, while others alter the small intestine to reduce the number of calories the body absorbs.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "These surgeries can also affect hormones, gut bacteria, appetite, and how the body metabolizes fat and uses insulin.",
      "sources": [1]
    },
    {
      "text": "Weight‑loss surgery is typically considered for adults with a body‑mass index of 35 or higher, especially when diet, exercise, or medications have not worked.",
      "sources": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "text": "It may also be offered to people with a BMI of 30‑35 who have serious obesity‑related health problems such as type 2 diabetes or sleep apnea.",
      "sources": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "text": "The goal is to achieve significant, sustained weight loss and improve obesity‑related conditions, though the procedures carry risks like infection, hernia, and blood clots.",
      "sources": [4]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "covered": true,
  "see_a_professional": false,
  "sentences": [
    {
      "text": "Weight‑loss surgery can cause immediate complications such as infection, bleeding, blood clots, and leaks at the surgical connections.",
      "sources": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Additional early problems include hernias at the incision site or inside the abdomen.",
      "sources": [1, 5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Later side effects may involve nutrient deficiencies leading to anemia or osteoporosis if prescribed vitamins are not taken.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Gallstones can develop after rapid weight loss, and some doctors prescribe medication for about six months to help prevent them.",
      "sources": [2]
    },
    {
      "text": "Long‑term issues can include strictures that narrow the stomach or connection, causing nausea, vomiting, and difficulty swallowing.",
      "sources": [5]
    },
    {
      "text": "Some patients may experience changes in alcohol metabolism after procedures like gastric bypass, increasing the risk of alcohol‑related problems.",
      "sources": [5]
    }
  ]
}
